{"groups":[{"members":["c1"],"primary":"c1","title":"California Tenant Protection Act: just cause required to terminate tenancies","requirement":"After a tenant has continuously and lawfully occupied a unit for 12 months (or 24 months if an adult tenant was added before an existing tenant reached 24 months), the owner may not terminate the tenancy without an at-fault or no-fault just cause stated in the written notice. For no-fault terminations, the owner must pay relocation assistance equal to one month's rent within 15 calendar days of serving the notice, or waive the final month's rent.","key_value":"Just cause after 12 months of occupancy (24 months if adult tenants were added); no-fault relocation assistance of one month's rent, paid within 15 calendar days of service of the notice, or a waiver of the final month's rent","status":"in_force","effective_date":"2024-04-01","end_date":"2030-01-01","citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1946.2","coverage_conditions":"Residential real property where the tenant has continuously and lawfully occupied the unit for the required period. It applies notwithstanding any other law, except for the listed exemptions. It does not apply to property subject to a local just cause ordinance adopted on or before September 1, 2019, or to a later local ordinance that is more protective.","exemptions":"Transient and tourist hotels; nonprofit hospitals, religious facilities, extended care and licensed residential care facilities for the elderly, and adult residential facilities; school or university dormitories; tenants sharing a bathroom or kitchen with an owner-occupant; single-family owner-occupied residences renting no more than two units or bedrooms, and mobilehomes; owner-occupied duplexes where the owner lived in one unit at the start of the tenancy; housing with a certificate of occupancy issued within the previous 15 years (not mobilehomes); and certain separately alienable property such as single-family homes and condominiums.","coverage_from":"c1","conflict_note":null},{"members":["c2"],"primary":"c2","title":"Housing Crisis Act: relocation for lower-income tenants displaced by demolition","requirement":"Lower-income households (80% of area median income or lower) displaced when protected units are demolished for new construction must receive relocation benefits consistent with Gov. Code Chapter 16 (§ 7260 et seq.). Where a local relocation requirement also applies, the owner must pay the higher amount.","key_value":null,"status":"in_force","effective_date":null,"end_date":null,"citation":"Cal. Gov. Code § 66300 et seq.","coverage_conditions":"Lower-income households displaced by demolition of protected units for new construction.","exemptions":null,"coverage_from":"c2","conflict_note":null},{"members":["c3","c4"],"primary":"c4","title":"California relocation payments for temporary capital improvement displacement of less than 20 days (Civ. Code § 1947.9)","requirement":"A tenant household temporarily displaced for less than 20 days for capital improvement work is owed a per-day relocation payment plus actual moving expenses. For these displacements, this section governs instead of San Francisco Rent Ordinance Section 37.9C.","key_value":"$446.00/day or $436.00/day (plus actual moving expenses); the current figure is unlabeled in the source","status":"in_force","effective_date":null,"end_date":null,"citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1947.9","coverage_conditions":"Temporary capital improvement evictions or displacements of less than 20 days (applied in place of San Francisco Rent Ordinance Section 37.9C).","exemptions":null,"coverage_from":"c4","conflict_note":"D083 (c4) lists two per-day amounts, $446.00/day and $436.00/day, without labeling their effective periods, so the currently applicable amount cannot be determined from the source. D082 (c3) gives no amount."}],"dropped":[]}
